        73.44099426269531,
        122.00001525878906,
        495.1180725097656,
        130.8468017578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Thinning chains in MCMC has been used in several papers; see for instance [8, 13, 25]. Thinning",
      "bbox": [
        85.39599609375,
        133.9550323486328,
        501.8923645019531,
        142.80181884765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "consists of taking every k-th sample instead of all of them in order to reduce autocorrelation. In",
      "bbox": [
        73.44099426269531,
        145.91004943847656,
        501.8826599121094,
        154.7568359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "[27] it is shown that although thinning chains in MCMC reduces autocorrelation between MCMC",
      "bbox": [
        73.44099426269531,
        157.8650665283203,
        501.9024963378906,
        166.71185302734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "samples, it also reduces the precision of the estimates (i.e. the average over a thinned sample set",
      "bbox": [
        73.44099426269531,
        169.82106018066406,
        501.82281494140625,
        178.6678466796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "has greater variance than the average over the unthinned sample) [16]. Therefore, thinning is not",
